<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miriam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miriam is described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> as the daughter of Amram and Jochebed and the older sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> and Aaron. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> calls her a prophetess, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> names her among the seven major female prophets of Israel. Tradition identifies her with the unnamed sister who watched the infant Moses on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. In Exodus she leads the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-the-sea">Song of the Sea</a>. In Numbers she and Aaron speak against Moses over his &quot;Cushite&quot; wife, and she is afflicted with a skin disease and shut out of the camp for seven days. The Torah records that she died and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> also has her follow the infant Moses.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miriam.jpg" alt="Miriam" width="330" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Window: Edward Burne-Jones 1886; Photo: Wojciech Dittwald · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:E.Burne-Jones_Miriam_St.Giles.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Her name is written Mīryām in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, and its literal sense is given as rebellion. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> she is called Miriam the Prophetess. The narrative of the infancy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> describes an unnamed sister watching as the child is placed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, and tradition identifies her as Miriam. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, too, she follows the baby Moses at her mother's request. When Asiya, the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a>, finds him and he refuses to be suckled by her, Miriam tells Pharaoh's household that she knows a woman who can nurse him, without revealing that the woman is his own mother.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In Exodus Miriam is called a prophetess when she leads the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-the-sea">Song of the Sea</a> after the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a>'s army at the Yam Suph. According to the Midrash, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> led the men out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and taught them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>, so Miriam led the women and taught them.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> were camped at Hazeroth, Miriam and Aaron spoke against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> because he had married an unnamed Ethiopian or Cushite woman, translations differing. God rebuked them, and Miriam was left white with a skin disease. Aaron asked forgiveness for her, Moses prayed, and God ordered that she be excluded from the camp for seven days, which was done. The Midrash reads the complaint as one about Moses separating from his wife Tzipora, whom they thought righteous, and Jewish sources treat the affliction as a punishment for slander. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> tells of another Cushite wife, Tharbis, but dates that marriage well before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-exodus">the Exodus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> states that the people settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a> and that Miriam died and was buried there. The next verse says there was no water for the congregation, and Jewish tradition explained the sequence by a well of Miriam that dried up at her death. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> says that three leaders led Israel and that in their merit the people received the well through Miriam, the clouds of glory through Aaron and the manna through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>. The Talmud names her among the seven major female prophets of Israel.</p><p>Some Jewish feminists place a cup of water for Miriam at the Seder beside the cup of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a>, a custom said to have begun in the 1980s in a Boston Rosh Chodesh group. Some Modern Orthodox Jews add a piece of fish to the Seder plate in her honour. In the Eastern Orthodox Church she is commemorated on the Sunday of the Forefathers, the Sunday of the Fathers and the Wednesday after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Miriam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
